  "live_records": {
    "q-39480": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39480",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2},\\,{u}_{3}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$, we need to find a fourth vector $x$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{3}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are three pivot columns ($1$st, $2$nd and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{4}$ is a free variable. From the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{4}$ and ${x}_{3}=-2{x}_{4}$, respectively. Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{4})+(-2{x}_{4})+{x}_{4}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2{x}_{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{4} \\\\ -{x}_{4} \\\\ -2{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{4}=1,$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$",
