    "q-140491": {
      "dom_id": "step-q140491",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Consider the **orthonormal** set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an **orthonormal** basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|?$\n\n$$\nU=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\sqrt{17}\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot {R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=2{R}_{2}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3},$ and from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=3,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}=\\sqrt{34}.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{17}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{17}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|+|{x}_{3}|=|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{34}}|+|\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{34}}|=\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{34}}.$",
